<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-describing-distributions">
  <title>Describing a Distribution</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Describe the shape of a distribution as skewed, symmetric, or uniform.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify the approximate center and spread of a distribution from a histogram.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize outliers as values falling outside the overall pattern.</p></li>
      <li><p>Write a paragraph summarizing a distribution, supported by percentages.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>Reading a histogram is a skill; writing down what you read is a
    separate one. This section builds a routine for turning a graph into a
    paragraph. The routine has four parts, always in the same order: shape,
    center, spread, and outliers.</p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-oscar-data">
    <title>A worked example: ages of Oscar winners</title>

    <p>Here are the ages of the actresses who won the Academy Award for Best
    Actress each year from <m>1970</m> through <m>2001</m><mdash/>thirty-two
    winners in all.</p>

    <p><m>34</m>, <m>34</m>, <m>26</m>, <m>37</m>, <m>42</m>, <m>41</m>,
    <m>35</m>, <m>31</m>, <m>41</m>, <m>33</m>, <m>30</m>, <m>74</m>,
    <m>33</m>, <m>49</m>, <m>38</m>, <m>61</m>, <m>21</m>, <m>41</m>,
    <m>26</m>, <m>80</m>, <m>42</m>, <m>29</m>, <m>33</m>, <m>35</m>,
    <m>45</m>, <m>49</m>, <m>39</m>, <m>34</m>, <m>26</m>, <m>25</m>,
    <m>33</m>, <m>35</m></p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-oscar-ages">
      <caption>Ages of Academy Award winners for Best Actress,
      <m>1970</m><ndash/><m>2001</m>, in bins of width <m>5</m>
      years.</caption>
      <image width="80%" xml:id="image-oscar-ages">
        <description>A histogram of the ages of 32 Best Actress winners. Bars
        rise sharply from age 20 to a peak of 9 winners in the bin from 30 to
        35, then fall away to the right. After age 50 the graph is empty
        except for three isolated bars of height one near ages 61, 74, and 80.
        The distribution is clearly skewed to the right.</description>
        <asymptote>
unitsize(0.145cm, 0.36cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));

real
[] L = {20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,80};
real
[] C = { 1, 5, 9, 6, 5, 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1};

for (int i = 0; i &lt; L.length; ++i) {
  if (C
[i] &gt; 0) {
    filldraw((L
[i],0)--(L
[i]+5,0)--(L
[i]+5,C
[i])--(L
[i],C
[i])--cycle,
             paleblue, black+0.4bp);
  }
}

draw((20,0)--(88,0), black+0.6bp);
for (real t = 20; t &lt;= 85; t += 5) {
  draw((t,0)--(t,-0.25), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", t), (t,-0.35), S);
}

draw((20,0)--(20,10), black+0.6bp);
for (real s = 0; s &lt;= 10; s += 2) {
  draw((20,s)--(19.1,s), black+0.4bp);
  label(format("%g", s), (18.6,s), W);
}

label("Age at time of award (years)", (54,-1.7));
label(rotate(90)*"Number of winners", (12.5,5));
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>Now work through the four parts in order.</p>

    <p><em>Shape.</em> The distribution of ages appears skewed to the right.
    Most winners are young: <m>91\%</m> (<m>29</m> of <m>32</m>) were under
    <m>50</m>, and <m>56\%</m> (<m>18</m> of <m>32</m>) were <m>35</m> or
    younger. The long thin tail stretching toward <m>80</m> is what makes the
    distribution right-skewed.</p>

    <p><em>Center.</em> The distribution appears centered between <m>30</m>
    and <m>35</m> years. That single bin holds <m>28\%</m> (<m>9</m> of
    <m>32</m>) of the winners, more than any other.</p>

    <p><em>Spread.</em> The ages run from about <m>20</m> to about
    <m>80</m>, so the overall range is approximately <m>60</m> years. There is
    a great deal of variability in the ages of Best Actress winners.</p>

    <p><em>Outliers.</em> Winners older than <m>60</m> are unusual. There are
    three such values, one each in the intervals
    <m>60</m><ndash/><m>65</m>, <m>70</m><ndash/><m>75</m>, and
    <m>80</m><ndash/><m>85</m>. Each is separated from the main body of the
    data by at least one empty bin.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-skew-direction">
      <statement>
        <p>Why is the distribution in <xref ref="fig-oscar-ages"/> described
        as skewed to the <em>right</em> rather than to the left?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The long, thin tail of the distribution extends toward the
            higher values on the right.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Skew is named for the direction of the tail, not the
            direction of the bulk of the data.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Most of the data is piled up on the left side of the
            graph.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is a true observation, and it does accompany right skew,
            but it is not the definition. Skew is named for the tail.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The tallest bar is to the right of the center of the
            graph.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The tallest bar is at <m>30</m> to <m>35</m>, well to the left
            of the midpoint of the axis.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The values increase from left to right along the axis.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is true of every histogram and has nothing to do with
            skew.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-outlier-meaning">
      <statement>
        <p>In this data set, the winner aged <m>80</m> is identified as an
        outlier. What does that mean?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Her age falls well outside the overall pattern of the rest of
            the data.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. An outlier is a value that stands apart from the
            pattern, separated from the main body of the distribution.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Her age was recorded incorrectly and should be removed.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>An outlier may be a recording error, but it may also be a
            perfectly real and interesting value. Jessica Tandy really did win
            at <m>80</m>. Never delete a value just because it is
            unusual.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>She is the oldest person in the data set.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>She is, but being the maximum does not by itself make a value
            an outlier. The <m>49</m>-year-old winners are near the top of the
            main group and are not outliers.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Her age is more than twice the mean age.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>No such rule defines an outlier. The judgment is about whether
            the value sits outside the overall pattern.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-writing-summary">
    <title>Turning observations into a paragraph</title>

    <p>Four separate observations are not yet a description. The next step is
    to assemble them into prose that someone who has not seen the graph could
    follow.</p>

    <p>Here is a plain-language version:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>Between <m>1970</m> and <m>2001</m>, the Oscar for Best Actress was
      awarded most often to young actresses: <m>56\%</m> (<m>18</m> of
      <m>32</m>) of the winners were <m>35</m> or younger, with <m>28\%</m>
      (<m>9</m> of <m>32</m>) between <m>30</m> and <m>35</m> years of age.
      Winners ranged in age from about <m>20</m> to about <m>80</m>, but only
      <m>3</m> of the <m>32</m> were over <m>60</m>.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>And here is the same description using the formal vocabulary of this
    chapter:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>Between <m>1970</m> and <m>2001</m>, the Oscar for Best Actress was
      awarded most often to young actresses. The distribution of ages is
      skewed to the right: <m>56\%</m> (<m>18</m> of <m>32</m>) of the winners
      were <m>35</m> or younger, with the center of the distribution between
      <m>30</m> and <m>35</m> years of age. With winners ranging in age from
      about <m>20</m> to about <m>80</m>, the overall range of the
      distribution is about <m>60</m>. But much of this variability is due to
      three outliers who were older than <m>60</m> when they won.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Both paragraphs are good. Notice what they have in common: every claim
    about the shape is backed by a number, and every number is attached to a
    unit and a group. <q>Skewed to the right</q> on its own is an assertion.
    <q>Skewed to the right: <m>56\%</m> of winners were <m>35</m> or
    younger</q> is evidence.</p>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-describe-checklist">
      <title>Describing a distribution</title>
      <p><em>Shape.</em> Skewed left, skewed right, symmetric with a central
      peak, or roughly uniform. Not every distribution fits one of these
      neatly<mdash/>say so when it does not.</p>
      <p><em>Center.</em> A typical value, or the interval where the data
      clusters.</p>
      <p><em>Spread.</em> How much the values vary. The overall range
      (largest minus smallest) is one measure; a typical range covering most
      of the data is another.</p>
      <p><em>Outliers.</em> Any values falling outside the overall pattern.</p>
      <p>Then write it as prose, supporting each claim with a percentage.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <!-- READING CHECK 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-percent-support">
      <statement>
        <p>Which of these is the strongest description of the Oscar age
        distribution?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><q>The distribution is skewed right; <m>29</m> of the
            <m>32</m> winners (<m>91\%</m>) were under <m>50</m>, with three
            older outliers at <m>61</m>, <m>74</m>, and <m>80</m>.</q></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. It names the shape, supports it with a percentage, and
            identifies the outliers specifically.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><q>The distribution is skewed right.</q></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>True but unsupported. A reader has no way to judge how
            pronounced the skew is.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><q>The bars are <m>1</m>, <m>5</m>, <m>9</m>, <m>6</m>,
            <m>5</m>, <m>3</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, <m>0</m>,
            <m>1</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>.</q></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This reports the graph without describing it. A description
            tells the reader what the pattern <em>means</em>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><q>Young actresses win more Oscars because Hollywood
            discriminates against older women.</q></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This leaps from description to causal explanation. The data
            shows the pattern; it does not establish why the pattern
            exists.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 4: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-write-description">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose a histogram of the amount students spent on textbooks this
        semester is roughly symmetric, centered near <m>\$300</m>, with values
        ranging from <m>\$50</m> to <m>\$550</m> and no gaps or isolated
        bars.</p>
        <p>Write a two- or three-sentence description of this distribution
        using the shape-center-spread-outliers routine.</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Something like: <q>The distribution of textbook spending is roughly
        symmetric with a single central peak. Spending is centered near
        <m>\$300</m>, and values range from about <m>\$50</m> to about
        <m>\$550</m>, an overall range of roughly <m>\$500</m>. There are no
        outliers<mdash/>no student's spending falls outside the overall
        pattern.</q></p>
        <p>Saying explicitly that there are no outliers is worth doing. A
        reader cannot tell the difference between <q>I checked and found
        none</q> and <q>I forgot to look</q> unless you say so.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-shape-vocabulary">
    <title>A vocabulary of shapes</title>

    <p>Four shapes come up often enough to have names.</p>

    <p>A distribution is <term>skewed to the right</term> when its tail
    extends toward larger values, as in <xref ref="fig-oscar-ages"/>. Income,
    home prices, and waiting times are commonly right-skewed, because there is
    usually a floor near zero but no ceiling.</p>

    <p>A distribution is <term>skewed to the left</term> when its tail
    extends toward smaller values. Scores on an easy exam often look this way:
    most students cluster near the top, with a few trailing down.</p>

    <p>A distribution is <term>symmetric with a central peak</term>, often
    called <term>bell-shaped</term>, when the two halves mirror each other
    around a single high point. Heights, and many measurement errors, look
    like this.</p>

    <p>A distribution is <term>uniform</term> when all bars are roughly the
    same height, meaning values are spread evenly across the range.</p>

    <p>Not every distribution fits one of these categories, and forcing a name
    onto a shape that does not have one is worse than describing it plainly.
    A distribution with two separate peaks, for instance, often signals that
    two different groups have been mixed together.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 5 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-shape-name">
      <statement>
        <p>A histogram of the time it takes customers to be served at a bank
        shows most customers served quickly, with a long tail of a few
        customers who waited a very long time. What is the shape?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Skewed to the right</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. The tail extends toward the large values, which is
            typical of waiting times: service cannot take less than zero time,
            but it can take a very long time.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Skewed to the left</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Left skew means the tail points toward <em>small</em> values.
            Here the unusual customers are the slow ones.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Symmetric with a central peak</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>A symmetric distribution would have as many unusually fast
            services as unusually slow ones. The long one-sided tail rules
            this out.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Uniform</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Uniform means the bars are all about the same height. Here most
            customers are concentrated at the short end.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 6 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-two-peaks">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>If a histogram does not match one of the four named shapes, the bin
        width must be wrong and should be adjusted until it does.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>False. Many real distributions genuinely do not have a simple
        shape, and adjusting the bins until the data matches a name you wanted
        is a way to mislead yourself. If a distribution has two peaks or an
        irregular pattern, describe what you actually see<mdash/>that
        irregularity is often the most interesting thing in the data.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <conclusion>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>To describe the distribution of a quantitative variable, report the
    overall pattern<mdash/>shape, center, and spread<mdash/>and any deviations
    from that pattern, meaning outliers.</p>
    <p>Shape may be skewed left, skewed right, symmetric with a central peak,
    or uniform, and some distributions fit none of these. Center is a typical
    value representing the group. Spread describes how the data varies; the
    overall range is one measure. Outliers are values falling outside the
    overall pattern.</p>
    <p>Assemble these observations into a paragraph, and support each claim
    about shape with a percentage. A description without numbers is an
    opinion.</p>
    <p>Adapted from <pubtitle>Concepts in Statistics</pubtitle>
    (SUNY / Lumen Learning), CC BY 4.0. Academy Award data from the Academy of
    Motion Picture Arts and Sciences.</p>
  </conclusion>
</section>